>
> Unfortunately, this is probably the least accurate thing you've said.
> 
> It's _very_ important who owns the name NetHack; the folks on the 
> DevTeam own it.  In my opinion, this is just obvious.
> 
> Any other package, regardless of whether it originates as a fork of 
> NetHack itself, is _not_ NetHack; it's something else.  Common usage in 
> the open source world would certainly allow a forked project to use its 
> parent project's name as _part_ of its own name, but certainly not to 
> claim the original name for its own.
> 
> NetHack is NetHack; anything else is not.  ;-)
> 
> -robin
> 
> (On the other hand, I'm all in favor of forks and variants; the more the 
> merrier, I think.  I'm just responding here to what seems to be a claim 
> that a forked project would have any right to call itself "NetHack".)


I've strongly stated earlier that it does matter who "owns" the name
"nethack", so in a practical sense I think we agree.  It matters
because the game can't be effectively developed by an alternate
development group until the current grip on officialdom is loosened,
bug reports are liberated, and in general the public can view nethack
from a newly structured dev project as the real deal.  There is a lot
of inertia to overcome.  Until then, the public will continue to go to
nethack.org to get what they perceive as official nethack

I disagree that rejuvenated nethack development must or should be done
in the context of forks and alternate names, though this may end up
being the only viable route if the current devteam would not
cooperate.  Such a project would be perceived by the player community
and the public as derivative and probably substandard work, and again
wouldn't get the exposure it deserves.  I've been proposing replacing
the current devteam structure because it hasn't been serving players
well and frankly, I believe, doesn't deserve to continue as the
central development authority.  I don't think more forking and
competing projects are the answer, for the above reasons and more
which I've previously discussed in this thread.

What have "Stichting Mathematisch Centrum and M. Stephenson" done
lately to deserve continued naming stranglehold on our "net-hacked"
game? - this is what we should be asking ourselves.  But if "NetHack
2007", "NetHack II", "NetHack:  The Updated More Frequently Than Every
3 Years Edition", or whatever name, is required to unify people and
get effective development going again, so be it.

Jim


